1
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:12,559
Veeva CRM lives in Salesforce. Vault CRM is a different
platform with its own objects,

2
00:00:12,559 --> 00:00:18,206
fields and API. This package moves the data between them — and
keeps both in sync until cutover.

3
00:00:18,206 --> 00:00:21,382
A run is planned as units — one per object × country —

4
00:00:21,382 --> 00:00:25,735
ordered so that every foreign key is loaded after the record
it points at.

5
00:00:25,735 --> 00:00:30,441
Each object module declares its field mapping. Config layers —
defaults, region,

6
00:00:30,441 --> 00:00:37,441
country — are folded into one materialised mapping per unit
and hashed, so a mapping change is detectable between runs.

7
00:00:37,441 --> 00:00:42,618
Preflight checks the plan against live metadata on both sides
before a single row moves:

8
00:00:42,618 --> 00:00:46,441
do the target fields exist, are the types and lengths
compatible,

9
00:00:46,441 --> 00:00:52,029
are the picklist values and object types real? Blocking
findings stop the run with exit code 2.

10
00:00:52,029 --> 00:00:55,735
Every mode takes --dry-run: extract and transform run for
real,

11
00:00:55,735 --> 00:01:00,088
the load is simulated. Nothing is written to Vault and no
watermark moves.

12
00:01:01,508 --> 00:01:05,861
The real init: extract by SOQL scope, transform through the
pure registry,

13
00:01:05,861 --> 00:01:09,567
then bulk-upsert into Vault keyed on the legacy Salesforce id
—

14
00:01:09,567 --> 00:01:13,450
so re-running the same load is idempotent rather than
duplicating.

15
00:01:13,450 --> 00:01:15,802
Which is real data in the target vault —

16
00:01:15,802 --> 00:01:20,508
Salesforce field names renamed to Vault ones, references
rewritten to Vault ids.

17
00:01:21,325 --> 00:01:27,148
Every migrated row is recorded in an id crosswalk. Foreign
keys are only ever resolved through it —

18
00:01:27,148 --> 00:01:33,266
never guessed — so a reference that has not been loaded yet
becomes a pending FK instead of a bad write.

19
00:01:33,266 --> 00:01:38,031
Cutover is not a single moment: the source keeps changing
while the project runs.

20
00:01:38,031 --> 00:01:44,325
So the tool runs deltas — a SystemModstamp window with overlap
and a safety lag, plus the deleted-row feed.

21
00:01:44,325 --> 00:01:48,207
A row whose source hash has not changed is never re-sent to
Vault.

22
00:01:48,207 --> 00:01:55,442
And the watermark only advances once a unit actually succeeds
— an interrupted run resumes its window instead of losing it.

23
00:01:55,442 --> 00:01:59,501
And the rename landed in Vault, on the same record — not a
duplicate.

24
00:02:02,434 --> 00:02:08,787
Before anyone signs off, verify re-counts both sides, checks
foreign keys for orphans and reads a stratified

25
00:02:08,787 --> 00:02:14,493
sample back out of Vault field by field. The cutover gate is
what turns that into a pass or fail.

26
00:02:14,493 --> 00:02:18,669
Every run writes a markdown and JSON report: config and
mapping hashes,

27
00:02:18,669 --> 00:02:22,904
per-unit timings, findings, reconciliation, and the ids of
anything that

28
00:02:22,904 --> 00:02:26,904
failed — which is what a regulated programme has to hand an
auditor.

29
00:02:26,904 --> 00:02:33,728
46 object modules, 1190 hermetic tests, no network — the whole
flow you just watched runs against fakes in a second.
